  "knowledgePattern": "Use the difference of same-side tangent radii in an auxiliary right triangle",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Add the radii at the tangent points",
      "body": "<p>Read the two centres and radii. Draw the common tangent with positive gradient, then draw a radius from each centre to its tangent point.</p>",
      "recap": "A radius at a tangent point is perpendicular to the tangent.",
      "pitfall": "The two circles lie on the same side of the line.",
      "pause": "Mark the centre-to-centre distance and both radius lengths.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b4-l03",
          "reminder": "A radius at a tangent point is perpendicular to the tangent."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Make a right-angled triangle",
      "body": "<p>From the smaller circle’s centre, draw a line parallel to the tangent until it meets the line containing the other radius. This gives a right-angled triangle between the two centres.</p>",
      "recap": "The two radii are parallel because both are perpendicular to the same tangent.",
      "pitfall": "The perpendicular side of your triangle uses the difference of the radii, not their sum.",
      "pause": "Find the remaining side using Pythagoras.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-right-triangle-ratios",
          "reminder": "Use Pythagoras in a right-angled triangle, keeping the hypotenuse opposite the right angle."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Turn the angle into a gradient",
      "body": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow></math> be the angle between the tangent and the positive horizontal direction. Find <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow></math> using your right-angled triangle.</p>",
      "recap": "A line’s gradient is rise divided by run, which is the tangent of its angle to the horizontal.",
      "pitfall": "Choose the positive gradient required by the question.",
      "pause": "Simplify the exact ratio and compare it with the options.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-straight-lines",
          "reminder": "A line’s gradient is the change in y divided by the change in x."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> The centres are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with radii 2 and 5. At each tangent contact, the radius is perpendicular to the tangent, so these two radii are parallel.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> Because both centres lie on the same side of the tangent, their separation perpendicular to it is the radius difference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Resolve the centre segment into its tangent-parallel and tangent-perpendicular parts. Pythagoras gives the parallel length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> If the positively sloping tangent makes angle θ with the horizontal, this auxiliary right triangle gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>6</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. This is the line gradient. Answer C.</p>"
